00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.780 align:start position:0%
 
I was wondering what would break first  I've been planning and researching this  post for a while if you want to train  like Batman or even just like an athlete  then preventing injury and optimizing  recovery is crucial the harder you push  yourself in workouts and the more varied  your training the more critical it is  that you balance that out with optimal  recovery and healing strategies knowing  this I've been employing multiple  strategies for many years to try and  ensure I do both but then a week ago I  put my back out and no it wasn't because  Bane broke me over one knee it was  significantly less awesome than that I  bent over an awkward angle while picking  up my 15 month daughter on a walk and it  caused a muscle strain in my lower back  then I came down with a mega cold which  is why my voice sounds a little bit  funny and no I don't think it's that  mega cold I thought for a while about  not writing this post after all I felt  like a bit of a hypocrite but then I  realized a few things firstly everyone  gets injured from time to time  there's simply no strategy that can  promise you will never get hurt or never  get ill and I wouldn't want to claim  otherwise also this was the perfect time  for me to assess what went wrong how it  could be prevented in future and how I  was going to accelerate my healing and  get back to training I'm learning along  with you guys it's all part of the  journey so I did even more research and  now I'm ready to suggest how Batman  might optimize his healing and prevent  injuries on the job and what all we mere  mortals could learn from that  [Music]  one of the ways I've always tried friend  injury and one of the tools Batman  should certainly employ is to strengthen  the tendons these are what connect our  muscles to our bones allowing us to  exert force through our joints  unfortunately they also adapt to  strength training in even anabolic  hormones much more slowly than muscle  does this is one reason that steljis can  actually lead to more injuries as the  muscles grow much faster than the  tendons can keep up whereas new lifters  can see structural changes within the  muscle in as little as eight days it  takes up to two months for similar  change to occur in tendons that makes it  important that you spend at least two  months building up a basic level of  strength before you start pushing  yourself with a serious progressive  overload it's also why Batman might  choose to use high volume calisthenics  as a method to increase blood supply to  both muscle and the tendons the issue is  that connective tissue doesn't have as  much blood supply as muscle during 300  press up several times a week for a few  weeks we'll actually increase the number  of capillaries supplying those muscles  and tendons with blood this can also aid  hypertrophy meaningly responds better to  training the future high volume  calisthenics also has the added bonus of  improving work capacity thereby  improving efficiency over time to  prevent sloppy form being introduced as  training goes on I recommend sticking  with simpler low stress exercises like  push-ups and pull-ups you can also vary  the specific movements you use between  workouts in order to minimize the amount  of repetition on the joints another  method I use is training for everything  I can by that I mean not just training  in a single plane of movement but  working on things like rotational  strength and mobility Batman should  certainly do the same by training with  calisthenics animal movements sandbags  kettlebells and more this is important  as some more rigid approaches to  training can otherwise actually compound  the issues the modern life already  introduces when you sit at a computer  all day hunched over you create  tightness and certain muscles like the  pecs and the hamstrings while  lengthening and weakening others the  same thing happens if you show  preferential treatment to certain body  parts if you only train the big three  lifts or worse the mirror muscles then  you'll build back some strength in areas  like the hamstrings but completely  ignore external rotation of the  shoulders rotational strength etc  rotational strength is not sexy but it's  crucial for every single sport and  certainly in combat whether you're  grappling or twisting your body to throw  a punch  this is the type of strength you need  likewise it's the strength you need when  moving furniture or when you turn to  pass something to someone behind you as  Pat McKenna Mara told Joe Rogan's in the  transverse plane lives life saving and  ass-kicking if you have let your  obliques become weak then sudden  movements in this position could result  in a tear or an injury and if you only  ever train the attractive muscles on the  front of your body and the work a desk  job then a shoulder injury is just  waiting to happen if you spend your days  with a whole lot of tension pulling your  body forwards all the time it has been  said that gymnasts are particularly  resilient against injury considering the  huge amount of high-impact training they  do I don't know how true that is as I've  also heard specific gymnasts a that they  find it unusual not to be in pain but  the point stands that they do incredible  things without breaking their backs  how by training in ways that many  conventional strength training methods  overlooked or even actively avoid Chris  summers of gymnastic bodies recommends  using the Jefferson curl to strengthen  the back for example this involves  actively rounding the spine under load  which can feel extremely unnatural to  those of us who have always been taught  to keep our backs neutral during most  lifts but the point is that your back  has mobility there it's designed to do  that you can avoid moving that way in  the gym but not in real life so why not  strengthen it the key is simply to know  what you're doing and I highly recommend  practicing with a coach and to start  with very light weights you can also  take a look at old-time strongmen for  demonstrations of this they would do  seemingly insane things like bending  backwards while pressing weights this  kind of thing might look bonkers but the  point is that by training at these  awkward angles they become stronger in  them they become ready for anything but  and this is a big one you absolutely  should not do anything that looks crazy  or dangerous without the help of a coach  and using very light weights to start  with while your body may be capable of  doing that it's not currently optimized  for it but if I knew all this then what  went wrong if I'm used to doing single  arm kettlebell clean and presses with my  thirty-six kilogram kettlebell why did  picking up my 13 kilogram daughter with  both hands take me down there are  several answers to that question one of  these is the specifics of the movement  I've developed twisting movements and  I've developed squatting movements but  in this case I was walking in one  direction twisting backwards to catch my  daughter who wanted to go the other way  all while bending down on an even  terrain I was spending over rather than  squatting because I didn't have much  time to react and that route is covered  in potholes that meant my backwards also  rounded I might have done the occasional  Jefferson curl and I might have trained  my rotational strength but both at the  same time with a very unusual angle of  resistance moving away from me first  thing in the morning when the cold wall  run down you get the picture  one option would be to focus on  combining movements and on transitioning  between movements a squat a deadlift or  a bicep curl are all examples of  constrained movements those are  one-dimensional repetitive movement  patterns in real life we rarely use  constraining from patterns but rather  these dynamically adapts to  ever-changing demands so one has to  solve this disparity is to use movement  training training systems like animal  flow are all about linking movements  together and transitioning through all  planes of motion they also include the  large amounts of spinal articulation and  rotation and no two workouts are ever  the same of course you don't have to  follow a predefined training program  like animal flow in order to get used to  using animal movements in your own  workouts trail running using barefoot  shoes can similarly prepare us for  uneven terrain and ground more of nuts  is built around this kind of philosophy  another option is to use more hybrid  exercises that combine two or more  movements into a single repetition and  discuss this in my most recent Batman  training program all we can go one step  further with another interesting  training protocol called loaded movement  training first described in this form by  Michael Dowell quartz Viper system VIP  are this type of training revolves  around task oriented movement patterns  under load this kind of training  challenges the body in a variety of  different ways and can help build that  legendary farmer strength that gives a  manual workers seemingly invincible  tendons and a bulletproof mentality it's  fantastic for building up tendon  strength and building more resilience  especially in your core  another factor is mobility this is an  issue I know I had I've been performing  pistol squats on the daily basis but if  you look at my form I actually have  quite a rounded back this is due to a  lack of mobility of my right ankle which  in turn is a hangover from a very old  injury and avulsion fracture caused by  an errant rabbit hole this prevented my  need from moving further forwards which  forced me to round my back to stay  upright now rounded back isn't actually  a serious crime when there's no load but  doing this daily mostly likely cost a  little bit of tension on my back prior  to the movement now we have in  everything that I spoke about before and  have a recipe for disaster this is just  one of the reasons that mobility should  be emphasized if you want to improve  your longevity in training  it's also why assessing your own Foreman  being honest about your own mistakes and  limitations is important to keep in mind  as well that limited mobility can be  acute as well as chronic an injury can  cause tension for example as can stress  this is why I recommend doing a short  diagnostic check before each workout you  can do this during a warm-up or even as  part of a body scan meditation which  also helped to strengthen the mind  muscle connection for further reduced  injury risk in short you check in with  each muscle group to see whether it's  feeling stiff or painful although it's  carrying any tension from there you can  then try to address those issues with  frame rolling or simply decide not to  push too hard in that area during a  given workout ultimately this is the  difference between treating training  light cube job if you can't afford to be  injured then you need to be slow and  deliberate in your training you need to  train for longevity not from your ego  that's when training becomes serious and  Batman is always serious that's it for  part 1 guys stay tuned for part 2 where  I'll be discussing recovery and don't  worry about me and my back I'm already  starting to exercise lightly once again  and I've nearly finished this cold  despite what my voice might sound like  hopefully by the next video I'll be ripe  as rain so thanks ton for watching guys  if you enjoyed this then please share it  around and like comment and subscribe  all those things to help me out  immensely and stay tuned for the next  one bye for now  oh and if you like this kind of approach  to training it incorporates lots of  different methodologies then you'll  probably enjoy my ebook and training  program super functional training you  find that in the link in the description  down below  you

